+Any script language can emit OSC 8 hyperlinks around individual text sections in
+its last output line. One command can return several independently clickable
+labels, mixed with ordinary text and separators. No per-link command or special
+JSON format is needed. For example, this Python producer emits two links:
+
+```python
+print(
+    "\x1b]8;;https://example.com/one\x07One\x1b]8;;\x07"
+    " | "
+    "\x1b]8;;https://example.com/two\x07Two\x1b]8;;\x07"
+)
+```
+
+Emit actual ESC (`0x1b`) bytes. Both BEL (`0x07`) and ST (`ESC` followed by `\`)
+are accepted terminators. Close each link with OSC 8 and an empty destination.
+Plain text continues to work. Invalid, unclosed, non-HTTP(S), or credential-bearing
+authority URLs display as plain text without activation. Other terminal controls
+are stripped. There is no separate link-count or 80-character label limit: the
+complete output line, including URLs and escapes, must fit in 4096 bytes.
+Never include API keys, access tokens, passwords, or other secrets in URLs.
+
+Click an underlined status text section to open its link. Status values and links
+do not appear in the main menu. Refreshing or viewing status never opens a link.
+
+Opening uses the attached client's browser, including when a command runs on a
+remote Sandbox server. Both server and client need the multi-span status support
+for activation. Compatible older endpoints still display plain labels, but do not
+activate the new span metadata. Updating the host alone does not update a running
+remote server.
+
 ### Outer terminal window title
 
 Herdr emulates the terminals in its panes, so an `OSC 0`/`OSC 2` title written inside a pane stops at Herdr. Herdr writes its own title to the terminal it runs in, which is what window managers and terminal tab bars read:
